From fc17fa9406248dea0daf4e852518d556a2b51710 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 21:39:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rwatch: don't false-alarm wan-gw when the first hop just
 filters ICMP

Field report: on a PPPoE line whose first hop (ISP ONT/modem at 192.168.100.1, or
the PPPoE peer) drops ICMP echo 100%, rwatch's first-hop check pinged wan0_gateway
and logged 'FAILURE detected: wan-gw' every few minutes even though the WAN was
fully up and passing traffic - alarming users over a non-fault.

Root cause: check #1 flagged wan-gw purely on ICMP failure, but a dropped ping is
not proof the WAN is down - plenty of first hops filter ICMP by policy.

Fix: corroborate with ASUS's own WAN state before calling it a fault. On ping
failure, read wan0_state_t (fallback wan_state_t); WAN_STATE_CONNECTED==2 means the
link is genuinely up. If connected, the first hop is merely ICMP-filtering - log an
informative one-shot note (throttled via /tmp marker, re-armed when ICMP returns or
the WAN drops) instead of a failure, and do NOT add wan-gw to FAIL. Only when the
WAN is NOT connected does wan-gw remain a real failure. A truly-down PPPoE flips
wan0_state_t to DISCONNECTED (wanduck), so real outages still alarm.

Net: ICMP-blackholing first hops stop producing phantom watchdog failures; genuine
WAN-down still detected. sh -n clean. NOT BUILT - rides a future rung; METAL OWED
(confirm the note logs once and no wan-gw FAILURE on the ICMP-filtered line).
---
 release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c b/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c
index 450292a967..a4ec5eb8ac 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c
@@ -91,9 +91,29 @@ static void rwatch_gen(void)
 	  "GDX=\"$(nvram get rwatch_gdx)\"\n"
 	  "FAIL=\"\"; CRIT=\"\"\n"
 	  "\n"
-	  "# 1) first-hop reachability (by IP, two tries)\n"
+	  "# 1) first-hop reachability (by IP, two tries). A failed ping is NOT proof\n"
+	  "#    the WAN is down: many ISP first hops (a PPPoE peer, an ONT/modem such as\n"
+	  "#    192.168.100.1) drop ICMP echo entirely while the link carries traffic\n"
+	  "#    fine. So corroborate with ASUS's own WAN state (wan0_state_t==2 =\n"
+	  "#    WAN_STATE_CONNECTED) before calling it a fault: only flag wan-gw when the\n"
+	  "#    ping fails AND the WAN is not connected. If the ping fails but the WAN is\n"
+	  "#    connected, the first hop is simply ICMP-filtering - log that once (so a\n"
+	  "#    user reading the log is not alarmed) and carry on. (field 2026-08-04)\n"
+	  "ICMPMARK=/tmp/rwatch_gw_icmp_filtered\n"
 	  "if [ -n \"$GW\" ] && [ \"$GW\" != \"0.0.0.0\" ]; then\n"
-	  "  ping -c1 -W3 \"$GW\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || ping -c1 -W3 \"$GW\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || FAIL=\"$FAIL wan-gw\"\n"
+	  "  if ping -c1 -W3 \"$GW\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || ping -c1 -W3 \"$GW\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n"
+	  "    rm -f \"$ICMPMARK\"\n"
+	  "  else\n"
+	  "    WS=\"$(nvram get wan0_state_t)\"; [ \"$WS\" = 2 ] || WS=\"$(nvram get wan_state_t)\"\n"
+	  "    if [ \"$WS\" = 2 ]; then\n"
+	  "      if [ ! -f \"$ICMPMARK\" ]; then\n"
+	  "        logger -t rwatch \"note: first hop $GW is not answering ICMP, but the WAN is connected - the first hop filters ICMP; this is normal, not a fault\"\n"
+	  "        : > \"$ICMPMARK\"\n"
+	  "      fi\n"
+	  "    else\n"
+	  "      FAIL=\"$FAIL wan-gw\"\n"
+	  "    fi\n"
+	  "  fi\n"
 	  "fi\n"
 	  "\n"
 	  "# 2) loopback DNS through dnsmasq (hosts-file name: no upstream dependency,\n"
-- 
2.25.1

